WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.800
You've set how fast you go live,
with every limit enforced for you.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.500
Now the proof that those limits hold.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.400
Built so you can verify it
instead of taking it on faith.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.933
By the end you'll know
what keeps your data yours,

00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.567
what proves the record wasn't rewritten,
and what no agent can do alone.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.033
Leo is about to connect the CRM, a
mailbox and an agent to one system.

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:21.833
The question is what stops it
going wrong, and how he'd know.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.567
First, separation. Every organization's
rows sit behind row-level security,

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:26.467
enforced by the database itself.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:28.800
The application connects
with a database role

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.867
that isn't allowed to bypass that fence.

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:31.900
So a bug in a screen,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.233
a query or an agent prompt can't
reach another organization's rows,

00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:37.900
because the database never returns
them. Your data stays yours.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.833
Private and team only
records are enforced as well,

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:41.100
rather than hidden on screen.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.533
Second, a record you can check.

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:45.867
Every operation, by a person or
an agent, goes into the audit log.

00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:48.200
Each entry is chained to the
one before it with a hash.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.267
Change an old entry, or remove one,
and everything after it stops matching.

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.733
So a reviewer can trust the
history without taking our word.

00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.800
Approvals carry the same trail: who
proposed, which agent was involved,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.367
who approved, and what was frozen.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.267
Third, secrets.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:02.733
Stored credentials are encrypted under
a master key that lives with you,

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:06.333
not in the database and not in a backup.
Connection credentials are sealed.

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:08.733
An agent key is shown
once, expires on its own,

00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.200
and can never create other keys.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.600
Your project folder holds no key
at all, only a reference to one.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.933
Fourth, the actions reserved for
people, and no setting changes that:

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:19.333
approving, signing a definition,
executing an approved action,

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:21.700
switching Dry Mode, creating
keys and assigning roles.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.333
An agent that tries is refused,
and the refusal is useful.

00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:26.700
It stops and names the
screen and the button.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.533
Even for people, the proposer
can't approve their own work,

00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:31.633
and neither can the
person an agent acts for.

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:32.933
Last, extensions.

00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:35.033
A package is signed, by us or by
a publisher you chose to trust.

00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.833
It declares what it may write, and
what it reaches out to is checked.

00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:42.233
Installing or enabling one is itself
a proposal that a person approves,

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:43.633
so nothing arrives on faith.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.267
Say it out loud: that's a
vendor grading its own homework.

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.767
So don't take our word. A
reviewer can check the chain.

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.100
The master key stays with you.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.033
And the database holds the fence
even when a screen has a bug.

00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:53.833
Here's how to check.

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:57.233
In the console: Approvals, then an
approval's timeline, and Settings,

00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:58.400
Audit log for the full chain.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.567
Or ask your agent: "Who
approved the last send,

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:01.533
and what exactly did they approve".

00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:04.467
Ask it to approve, and it stops
and sends you to the right screen.

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:05.667
One habit worth copying:

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:07.167
pick one approval a month and compare

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.200
its frozen payload with
what actually went out.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.733
None of this replaces your judgment.

00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:13.633
Keep your master key somewhere safe,
review who holds approval rights,

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.500
and use Dry Mode while
you learn the system.

00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:19.400
Boiled down: a fence the database
enforces, a log that shows tampering,

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.267
and actions only people take.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.333
So you can hand an agent real
access and still prove what it did.

00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:26.433
Open the audit log and
read one entry today.

00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:29.767
The next clip is Dashboards, your
daily rhythm and what it costs:

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:31.833
how you run all of this in minutes a day.
